WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.761
Modern C++ provides abstractions that reduce exposure to manual buffer mistakes.

2
00:00:03.761 --> 00:00:09.573
These abstractions do not make every defect impossible, but they carry more information than raw pointers alone.

3
00:00:09.573 --> 00:00:15.727
They also make code easier to review because ownership, size, and lifetime are often visible in the type.

4
00:00:15.727 --> 00:00:21.198
`std::array` is useful for fixed-size storage where the size is part of the type.

5
00:00:21.198 --> 00:00:25.984
It works well when capacity is known at compile time and should not change.

6
00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:31.796
Reviewers can see that the object owns its storage and that the number of elements is stable.

7
00:00:31.796 --> 00:00:34.532
`std::vector` is useful for dynamic contiguous storage.

8
00:00:34.532 --> 00:00:41.712
It manages allocation and deallocation through RAII, carries its size and capacity, and supports operations that keep storage ownership inside the

9
00:00:41.712 --> 00:00:42.053
container.

10
00:00:42.053 --> 00:00:46.840
Code still needs range checks and invalidation awareness, but manual allocation pressure is lower.

11
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:50.259
`std::string` should generally be used for owned text data.

12
00:00:50.259 --> 00:00:53.336
It keeps length with the characters and manages storage.

13
00:00:53.336 --> 00:01:02.909
It is not a binary buffer, and it does not remove the need to understand encoding or output limits, but it is clearer than a raw character pointer

14
00:01:02.909 --> 00:01:03.935
for ordinary text.

15
00:01:03.935 --> 00:01:07.012
`std::span` is useful for bounded non-owning views.

16
00:01:07.012 --> 00:01:11.457
It lets an API accept a view of contiguous data without taking ownership.

17
00:01:11.457 --> 00:01:18.295
Because it carries a pointer and length together, it is usually easier to review than separate pointer and count parameters.

18
00:01:18.295 --> 00:01:21.030
The owner still has to outlive the span.

19
00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:23.081
RAII keeps ownership and lifetime clear.

20
00:01:23.081 --> 00:01:27.526
Containers, smart pointers, and small wrapper types release resources when objects leave scope.

21
00:01:27.526 --> 00:01:33.680
Buffer safety improves when the code does not rely on every branch manually releasing or resizing storage correctly.
